#
# arch/os/macos — macOS process services.
#
# The counterpart of arch/os/linux, and the same three questions: how a program
# with no C library under it reaches the kernel, how it draws randomness, and
# where the system call instructions in a run of code are.
#
# The fourth — arming those instructions in this process and standing behind the
# trap — has no entry here, because macOS has no answer to it. README.md in this
# directory says why at length; the short of it is that the text a program would
# want to arm is libsystem's, libsystem's text lives in the dyld shared cache,
# and the shared cache is mapped read-only, shared between every process on the
# machine and validated against its code signature. There is no CONFIG_OS_SC on
# this platform rather than one that returns an error.
#
menu "macOS process services"
	depends on PLATFORM = "macos"

config OS_IO
	bool "the system call, without a C library to make it through"
	default y
	help
	  _syscall6() and the calls a program makes with it, one _sys_ name
	  each: _sys_read, _sys_write, _sys_open, _sys_close, _sys_readlink,
	  _sys_access, _sys_execve, _sys_exit, _sys_getpid, _sys_getppid,
	  _sys_gettid, _sys_gettimeofday, _sys_sendto, _sys_recvfrom,
	  _sys_stat. The string helpers beside them are shared with every other
	  platform layer and live in <arch/os/str.h>: xstr* macros that resolve
	  to the C library's strlen/strcmp/... where a build has one and to the
	  hand-written nolibc_ loops where it does not, plus nolibc_utoa and
	  nolibc_say. That is why this directory is built -fno-builtin:
	  otherwise the compiler turns a nolibc_ loop back into the library
	  call it was written to replace.

	  Two things here are macOS and not Linux, and io.h says both where the
	  asm is. The number goes in x16 on arm64 rather than x8, and on x86-64
	  it carries the UNIX class in its top byte, because Darwin multiplexes
	  several call tables through one instruction. And the error comes back
	  as a positive number with the carry flag set, the way the BSDs have
	  always returned one — so the flag is read with the result and folded
	  into it, and every caller above this line goes on reading a negative
	  return as the kernel's error the way it does on Linux.

	  hpc's logger is the caller that matters on this platform: its open
	  and its write go through here whenever this symbol is set
	  (hpc/log/write.c), so a program with no C library can still log, and
	  so an object that interposes on write does not catch its own log
	  lines. Formatting stays the library's.

config OS_ENTROPY
	bool "the entropy system call, without a C library to make it through"
	default y
	help
	  _sys_getrandom(), made the same freestanding way arch/os/macos/io
	  makes _sys_read and the rest — one inline system call — so an object
	  with no library under it has a way to draw randomness and a fallback
	  for when the symbol it wraps could not be resolved.

	  The name is getrandom's and the call underneath is getentropy(2),
	  which is the one this kernel has. Two things are made up to keep the
	  Linux shape the callers are written against: the flags argument is
	  accepted and ignored, because there is no pool here to have opinions
	  about, and the 256-byte limit getentropy fails a longer call for is a
	  loop rather than an error.

	  Beside it is the entropy seam,
	  <arch/os/macos/entropy/hooks.h>: one macro, entropy_hook_fill(),
	  handed the buffer a source just filled, free to read it or to rewrite
	  it in place before the program sees it. Off by default, and turned
	  into something by the header a build names below.

	  Say N and neither the wrapper nor the seam is in the tree.

config OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	bool
	depends on OS_ENTROPY
	help
	  Whether the entropy seam is asked for. Left to a consumer to turn on,
	  because a seam nothing reads is a call to nothing. With it on, the
	  header below names where the definitions come from.

config OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS_HEADER
	string
	depends on OS_ENTROPY_HOOKS
	help
	  The header <arch/os/macos/entropy/hooks.h> includes, written as it
	  would be inside the angle brackets — a path against one of the
	  build's header roots ("modules/ub/entropy/entropy.h"). Whatever it
	  does not define falls back to the same nothing.

config OS_SCAN
	bool "the syscall instruction sites in a run of code"
	depends on ARCH = "arm64"
	default y
	help
	  scan_run(): given a run of machine code and a list of offsets known
	  to be instruction boundaries, say where in it the system call
	  instructions are and, where the preceding instruction settled it,
	  which number each one makes.

	  Instructions are four bytes and aligned on arm64, so `svc #0x80` is
	  one word with nothing else to be mistaken for and the sweep is the
	  word loop in scan/arm64.c. Two constants are Darwin's rather than
	  Linux's: that trap immediate, and x16 as the register the number is
	  loaded into.

	  It is pure: no system calls, no allocation, no state, and nothing in
	  it cares whether the bytes came from a mapping or from a file.
	  Nothing in this tree links it on macOS yet — there is no in-process
	  half here to arm what it finds, and tools/ub-patch is an ELF editor —
	  so it is built for what it is, one answer to a question about bytes.

	  x86-64 is not offered. The two-byte `syscall` there occurs inside
	  immediates and displacements and needs the instruction length decoder
	  arch/os/linux/scan carries to tell a site from a coincidence, and
	  there is no consumer on this platform to have written a second one
	  for.

endmenu
